# Handoff — 2026-08-07: cli-quality-of-life SHIPPED (commit e880560)

## Session summary

This session continued from where `cli-consumer-ux` was shipped (commit `1a6bfca`). The user explicitly asked me to start the `cli-quality-of-life` pack to close four open drifts (D-78, D-81, D-83, D-84) that the cli-consumer-ux pack intentionally deferred (their drift entries said "Owner: agentide CLI pack (next)"). All four are now closed.

**Pipeline path:** GRILL → PRD-TRD → IMPL → Implement (TDD, 4 phases) → post-impl sim → drift check (sub-agent) → drift.md housekeeping → this handoff. Backlog update is still pending (see "What the next session should do" #4).

## What was built — the four drifts, in detail

### D-78 (High) — `agentide init --data-dir <fresh>` no longer fails

**Problem:** `agentide init --data-dir /tmp/foo` returned `ENOENT: no such file or directory, open '/tmp/foo/gateway-secret'` and exit 1. The operator had to run `mkdir -p` first. The CLI was supposed to be the bootstrap path — it wasn't self-sufficient.

**Fix:**
- Added an **optional** `mkdir?(path, options?: { recursive?: boolean })` method to the `FileSystem` interface in `packages/gateway-core/src/types.ts:152`. Making it optional (not required) keeps every existing FileSystem fake compatible — they all omit the method and pass type checking.
- Implemented it in `packages/gateway-core/src/factory.ts:712-714` (`nodeFileSystem().mkdir`) and `packages/agentide/src/bin.ts:33-36` (the bundled CLI's `defaultFs.mkdir`). Both use `node:fs/promises.mkdir`.
- `packages/agentide/src/cli.ts:runInit` (L300-302) calls `opts.fs.mkdir(dataDir, { recursive: true })` if `opts.fs.mkdir` is a function (production fs does — in-memory fakes don't, but that's fine, they don't write to disk anyway).

**Why the seam and not just `node:fs/promises.mkdir` directly:** the CLI's bundled binary has its OWN `defaultFs` in `bin.ts`, separate from gateway-core's `nodeFileSystem`. The InMemoryFs (tests) doesn't track directories. Both production paths needed the method added — one at `bin.ts:33-36`, one at `factory.ts:712-714`. The seam keeps test fakes working without modification.

**Test:** `packages/agentide/src/__tests__/cli-init.test.ts` — 3 cases. Uses a `HybridFs` that real-mkdirs on disk but keeps file content in-memory. This is the right boundary for the test: the dir creation is observable on the real fs, the file content lives in the test.

### D-81 (Medium) — `agentide status` recovers data-dir from pid file

**Problem:** `agentide status` (no `--data-dir`) defaulted to `cwd + '/.agentide/data'`. When the gateway was started with `--data-dir=/tmp/foo` and the operator ran `status` from a different cwd, status failed with `ENOENT ./ .agentide/data/gateway-secret`.

**Fix:**
- `packages/agentide/src/lifecycle.ts:writePidFile` (L111-117) now writes JSON `{"pid":1234,"dataDir":"/tmp/foo","startedAt":"2026-08-06T..."}` instead of just `1234\n`.
- `packages/agentide/src/lifecycle.ts:readPidFile` (L119-141) returns a discriminated union: `null | {pid, dataDir?, startedAt?} | {pid}` (the third branch is the legacy plain-number fallback for old pid files). Tries JSON parse first; falls back to `parseInt` if the file starts with a digit.
- `packages/agentide/src/start.ts:runDetachedStart:340` calls `writePidFile(pidFile, detached.childPid, dataDir, new Date().toISOString())`.
- `packages/agentide/src/cli.ts:runStatus` (L341-366) accepts an optional `pidFile` arg. If present and the pid file carries `dataDir`, it overrides the cwd-relative default. The `case "status"` branch in `runCli` (L249) wires the default pid file path.

**Test:** `packages/agentide/src/__tests__/lifecycle-pidfile.test.ts` — 5 cases (write+read JSON round-trip, legacy plain-number fallback, malformed → null, missing → null, `removePidFile` idempotency).

**Also updated:** `packages/agentide/src/__tests__/detached-start.test.ts:57-63` — the test's local `readPid` helper now parses JSON. Without this, the test broke because `parseInt` on `{"pid":...}` returns NaN.

### D-83 (Low) — `agentide stop` unifies on rc 0

**Problem:** `agentide stop` exited 0 for "pid file present + pid dead" but rc 1 for "no pid file". Both states mean "nothing to stop" — the inconsistency broke `agentide stop && next` shell chains.

**Fix:**
- `packages/agentide/src/start.ts:runStop` (L259) — one-character change: `return result("", ..., 1)` → `return result("", ..., 0)` in the missing-pid-file branch. The pid-dead branch was already rc 0 (via `result(${msgs[outcome]})` which defaults to exitCode 0).
- The two stderr messages still differ so the operator gets the right diagnostic.

**Test:** `packages/agentide/src/__tests__/cli-stop.test.ts` — 2 cases (both branches exit 0). The IMPL said "no new tests" (covered by existing `detached-start.test.ts`); I added the tests as a small upgrade because pinning rc 0 in two cases is more durable than relying on the larger test to exercise both.

### D-84 (Low) — per-subcommand help for `agentide client`

**Problem:** `agentide client --help` dumped six subcommands in one row without per-subcommand flag details. `client grant` legitimately needs `--tenant/--name` (not `--client-id/--scope` like the rest). Operator guessed.

**Fix:**
- New `clientHelp(sub?: string)` function in `packages/agentide/src/cli.ts:460-481` returns either the six-row summary (`sub === undefined`) or the per-subcommand flag block.
- `packages/agentide/src/cli.ts:runClient` (L491-493) short-circuits on `sub === undefined || flags["help"] === true` and returns `result(clientHelp(sub))`. Exit 0.
- `packages/agentide/src/cli.ts:runCli` (L231) — the top-level `--help` gate was the bug: it caught `flags["help"] === true` before `runClient` saw it, so `agentide client grant --help` printed the full top-level help instead of the grant-specific help. Fix: only short-circuit when `cmd === undefined` (or `cmd === "--help"`/`"-h"`). Now `agentide client grant --help` flows into `runClient` and gets the dispatcher.

**Test:** `packages/agentide/src/__tests__/cli-client-help.test.ts` — 4 cases (no-subcommand, `grant --help`, `create --help`, `redeem --help`).

